    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. So the risk transfers at the shipment port, not the destination. Regarding the insurance part—you mentioned the seller pays for it. Does the seller have any control over the terms of that insurance, or is the coverage level strictly defined by Incoterms 2020?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit on a crucial detail, though it's worth noting that the seller's control over the insurance terms is often more limited than you might think, especially when dealing with high-value items like electronic components. Under CIF 2020, the seller is required to procure insurance covering the goods for their \"institute cargo clauses\" (usually Institute Cargo Clauses (C) or similar minimum coverage), which typically covers \"named perils\" rather than \"all risks.\"\n\nThis means the seller doesn't get to pick the coverage level arbitrarily; they must meet the minimum statutory requirements of the Incoterms. However, they *do* have the option to buy additional insurance if they wish, but any extra coverage beyond the minimum must be explicitly agreed upon in the contract. It's a bit like a standard homeowner's policy that covers fire and theft, but if you want flood coverage, you'd need to add an endorsement and pay extra.\n\nIf the seller tries to pass the cost of this mandatory insurance onto the buyer without clear agreement, it could lead to a dispute, so it's best to specify the exact insurance clause in your contract. Also, remember that the insurance must be in the buyer's name, or at least allow",
      "source": "target",
